WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:04.080
Preparing for Winter
Barbara Hakin

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.410
Deputy Chief Executive
Preamble

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:12.670
This paper is to promote a discussion about
winter with the board and to give reassurance

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:15.509
for our plans to manage the winter.

00:00:15.509 --> 00:00:22.109
None of us should underestimate the challenges
the NHS faces in the winter period compared

00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:29.099
to the summer months, and we expect this winter
to be particularly difficult because we know

00:00:29.099 --> 00:00:33.329
that the NHS is already stretched.

00:00:33.329 --> 00:00:38.829
More patients get treated, people are living
longer, there is more that we can do for those

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:44.180
patients, and in a time of economic restraint
we have to be extremely efficient in order

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.320
to maintain the high level of service we want.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:53.140
There has been a great deal of media interest
in winter; a great deal of it very negative.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:59.560
While I would not want in any way to underestimate
the challenge we face, I also think it is

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:04.720
absolutely important that everyone understands
the amount of effort that goes in across the

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.750
NHS to plan and prepare for winter, which
is something we have done for years and years

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:10.790
and years.

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:15.450
This winter we have redoubled our efforts
and we have to be particularly vigilant.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:23.360
That planning goes on within our individual
hospitals and GP surgeries, across communities

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.490
and across the wider systems.

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:26.610
A&amp;E
Four hours

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:31.010
The accident and emergency departments are
always a focus of attention.

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:36.250
That is because they tend to be the pressure
point of winter.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:41.180
When the system is not running as efficiently
or effectively as it could, we tend to see

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:46.710
the problems in our A&amp;E departments with patients
having to wait too long.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:53.780
I remind people about the standard of everyone
being treated -- having their completed episode

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.220
in A&amp;E -- within four hours.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:59.210
Again, I think some people think it is about
waiting four hours to be seen, which it most

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:00.210
certainly is not.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:04.700
It is that at the end of four hours you are
either discharged, if you do not need any

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:08.910
further treatment, or you will have left A&amp;E
and been admitted to hospital.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:17.090
Again, I think that in some instances people
think that the patients who are being discharged

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:21.340
are the ones who often wait more than four
hours, and that is not actually the case,

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.250
although that does happen.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:29.080
The biggest difficulty we have, and why the
A&amp;E standard is such a good barometer for

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:33.500
us, is that the patients who have to wait
longer than four hours are those patients

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:38.160
who are sick and who are waiting for a bed,
which makes it all the more unacceptable but

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:46.420
actually makes it evident that this is a feature
of a system issue and an issue within the

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:47.420
hospital.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:49.320
Prevention
Whilst there are not actually more patients

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:55.850
attending A&amp;E or being admitted to hospital
than there were at this time last year, it

00:02:55.850 --> 00:03:02.020
would appear that the patients who are admitted
are sicker and that there is more difficulty

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:05.700
in supporting these patients to be discharged.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:13.360
Often what we see in A&amp;E is simply a feature
of the fact that our hospitals are not able

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.950
to empty their beds to find patients, to help
patients back into the community, which is

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:19.800
of course where patients need to be.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:25.030
Therefore in our efforts in planning for the
winter, while what happens within the hospitals

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:30.940
and within the A&amp;E department itself is critical,
it is equally essential that we put in place

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:35.170
all those things that will mean that patients
will not need to go to hospitals if they do

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:38.980
not have to -- do not need to be admitted
-- and similarly that they can be discharged

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.600
as soon as they are ready to go home.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:46.310
Often, that is not just a feature the NHS
can put in place, but critically also the

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:51.010
social care services that are available to
them.

00:03:51.010 --> 00:04:00.630
We monitor the A&amp;E standard on a weekly basis
in order to identify how things are going,

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:04.990
and I am pleased to report that we have seen
an improvement in that standard over the last

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:10.970
week, with the majority of places being able
to treat patients quickly and efficiently.

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:15.251
Local activities
We have a number of strands to what we are

00:04:15.251 --> 00:04:22.400
doing in order to keep us prepared for winter
and make sure that we can maintain this standard.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:23.960
Key and critical is what is going on locally.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:27.940
We all know that that is what makes a difference
to patients -- what their local services are

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:32.410
like -- and that it is local clinicians and
managers and patients and the public supporting

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:36.470
them that can make the difference, not necessarily
the things we do nationally.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:42.940
We have urgent care working groups with their
winter plans.

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:49.430
These are, on a day-to-day basis, identifying
what needs to change to make the service more

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:50.430
efficient.

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:55.220
As we move into the winter, I would expect
that these groups will become much more operational

00:04:55.220 --> 00:05:00.410
and much more able, on a week-to-week basis,
to make sure that the right capacity is in

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:02.310
the right place for patients.

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:05.941
National activities
We also have a lot of activity going on at

00:05:05.941 --> 00:05:07.990
a national level.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:12.630
Obviously the paper describes the additional
funding that has gone to the health communities

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:15.970
that we thought would have the biggest struggles.

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:24.250
There is a lot of work going on across our
cells and local government and across the

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:29.150
NHS trust development organisation and Monitor,
the two organisations that oversee hospitals,

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:33.900
to ensure that we look at the urgent care
plans and we look at the places where there

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:39.860
are hotspots on a weekly basis; that we focus
on which hospitals are having the most problems;

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:45.949
that we focus on the urgent care plans for
those places and make sure they have looked

00:05:45.949 --> 00:05:51.530
at everything they could do to support that
hospital to deliver timely services for patients.

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:53.600
Urgent care plans
The vast majority of the urgent care plans

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:54.600
are excellent.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:59.660
I have some concerns that some of them are
a little inwardly focused, and what we will

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:04.560
be doing is encouraging the CCGs and the local
providers to make sure that they are working

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.810
with the ambulance trusts, who have a major
part to play, and the independent sector and

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:15.190
the voluntary sector, who can often be really
important for helping patients during the

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:16.350
winter.

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:23.510
However, nationally our attention to that
focus on how individual health economies are

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:24.940
doing is significant.

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:31.000
We also have a major role to play nationally
in terms of understanding what is going on.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:36.300
The paper describes the 'sitreps' that we
now have in place, which give us a really

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:43.270
granular view of what is happening across
the system and also allow us to make surges

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:48.820
of demand where it changes geographically,
making sure that, particularly for those services

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:52.570
that serve a very small number of patients
-- paediatrics, intensive care beds, ECMO

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:58.639
beds -- that we can flex and make those available
to patients across different parts of the

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:02.750
country as there are different issues in different
places.

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:05.470
111
Another major issue for dealing with winter

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:12.759
will be the 111 service, and I use this opportunity
to just report to the board that 111 continues

00:07:12.759 --> 00:07:19.150
to deliver a good service for patients; it
continues to meet the standards that we are

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:27.350
expecting in terms of answering calls and
passing patients onto clinicians to be treated.

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:32.990
We continue to get very high levels of patient
satisfaction, but I think we all hear anecdotes

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:36.380
now of patients who have called 111 and are
very pleased with the service.

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:42.229
Sadly this does not seem to be reflected in
media coverage of 111, given the negative

00:07:42.229 --> 00:07:44.790
coverage that it had initially.

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:48.449
Nevertheless, again, even with 111, we are
pleased with the progress we have made, but

00:07:48.449 --> 00:07:52.789
that does not mean that we do not have to
be extremely vigilant because 111 will become

00:07:52.789 --> 00:07:57.950
busier over the winter, and I hope it will
play a major part in supporting us during

00:07:57.950 --> 00:07:59.230
that time.

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:07.350
The paper describes the changes that we are
seeing because NHS Direct is leaving the market

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:09.610
of 111.

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:15.910
Already we have had a number of transfers
to new providers, dominantly ambulance trusts

00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:24.160
of the North West, and Somerset have already
gone live on the transfers to those ambulance

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:25.160
trusts.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:28.320
Over the next few weeks we will see significantly
more transfers.

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:33.300
We are also seeing new services in 111 and
extensions of services coming through all

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:34.329
the time.

00:08:34.329 --> 00:08:40.579
What I can reassure the board is that our
oversight of that process is very significant

00:08:40.579 --> 00:08:46.420
indeed and really robust, including asking
the CCGs commissioners to provide us with

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:52.769
external assurance that the new providers
are able to take on the service and not have

00:08:52.769 --> 00:08:54.630
a dip in performance.

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:59.300
The ones where we have seen a transfer, there
has been no dip in performance, which is very

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:03.139
reassuring, but we will continue to oversee
that.

00:09:03.139 --> 00:09:09.459
In fact, last week I was party to a decision
not to stand up a new provider because we

00:09:09.459 --> 00:09:13.029
thought that we did not have the assurance
that they were ready, and we therefore made

00:09:13.029 --> 00:09:14.470
alternative arrangements.

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:18.670
Communications campaign
Finally, just to say that what will be important

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:25.560
for us over the winter, I think, will be getting
the right communications campaign with public

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:26.850
and the media.

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:31.709
I think there are three strands to the campaign
for us.

00:09:31.709 --> 00:09:35.079
Keeping well
The first one is communicating with the public

00:09:35.079 --> 00:09:40.450
about what they can do to keep themselves
well; what they can do so that they do not

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:46.910
need NHS services over the winter, and also
where they get those services and what is

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:51.970
the easiest route for them to find an NHS
service if they need one.

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:54.550
So that will be one important part.

00:09:54.550 --> 00:10:03.259
It will include NHS England doing a significant
media campaign about how people can keep well

00:10:03.259 --> 00:10:08.839
in winter, and obviously that will be tied
in with the flu campaign.

00:10:08.839 --> 00:10:13.089
We should not underestimate the important
of flu vaccinations at this time, both for

00:10:13.089 --> 00:10:18.379
our own staff -- where we have a big campaign
-- but also for those patients who are eligible.

00:10:18.379 --> 00:10:24.079
And it is good to report that the latest figures
show that already significantly more than

00:10:24.079 --> 00:10:30.239
half of the patients over 65 who are eligible
for a flu injection have had one, and a third

00:10:30.239 --> 00:10:34.749
of patients with long-term conditions, and
we are seeing good progress in the other categories

00:10:34.749 --> 00:10:39.179
of pregnant women and children in their flu
vaccinations.

00:10:39.179 --> 00:10:45.149
Winter Heroes campaign
As well as the campaign for the how to keep

00:10:45.149 --> 00:10:49.529
well, we obviously will be supporting the
Winter Heroes campaign, because often in the

00:10:49.529 --> 00:10:55.769
winter it is isolation and the elderly patients
who have not got anybody to turn to help them

00:10:55.769 --> 00:10:59.149
with simple things in the winter, which leads
to a deterioration in health.

00:10:59.149 --> 00:11:02.009
So we will be supporting that Winter Heroes
campaign.

00:11:02.009 --> 00:11:04.339
Communicating information
The second part of our campaign is that actually

00:11:04.339 --> 00:11:08.070
we should tell people what is happening, to
make sure that people really do understand

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:12.769
the facts about what is going on in any department
and what is going on in winter services.

00:11:12.769 --> 00:11:18.970
Sometimes some of the media have a tendency
to exaggerate the negative, so we need to

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:24.449
make sure that the public are not unreasonably
frightened or cause concern where that is

00:11:24.449 --> 00:11:25.449
not necessary.

00:11:25.449 --> 00:11:30.959
However, we need to be scrupulously honest
and very clear where there are problems and

00:11:30.959 --> 00:11:31.959
issue.

00:11:31.959 --> 00:11:35.980
For the most part, though, we can say that
our services are working well, and we should

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:40.389
keep the public reassured of all the things
we are doing, all the plans we are making,

00:11:40.389 --> 00:11:41.619
to keep the service safe.

00:11:41.619 --> 00:11:45.260
Understanding the future
The final part will be about helping the public

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:51.249
to understand the future -- looking forward,
based on Sir Bruce's Urgent Care Review, and

00:11:51.249 --> 00:11:56.389
how we see, as the years go by, that we will
be able to continue to provide this vitally

00:11:56.389 --> 00:11:57.939
important urgent care service.

00:11:57.939 --> 00:11:59.779
Urgent Care Review
Bruce Keogh

00:11:59.779 --> 00:12:07.420
Medical Director
History of urgent care

00:12:07.420 --> 00:12:12.790
I think that, to put it in context, despite
some occasional and obvious problems, I think

00:12:12.790 --> 00:12:18.080
we should be very proud of our NHS and the
social values that underpin it and that it

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:19.429
represents.

00:12:19.429 --> 00:12:23.439
No more so, I think, than when somebody needs
urgent care; when they are frightened, when

00:12:23.439 --> 00:12:27.559
they are anxious, when they are in physical
or mental pain.

00:12:27.559 --> 00:12:34.209
We have many examples of how this is delivered
really well, effectively and efficiently around

00:12:34.209 --> 00:12:35.299
our NHS.

00:12:35.299 --> 00:12:43.679
However, it is strange that the emergency
care system at a national level has not been

00:12:43.679 --> 00:12:47.259
systematically reviewed since the early 1970s.

00:12:47.259 --> 00:12:53.299
Yet despite that, science and technology have
advanced in that time; they have had a major

00:12:53.299 --> 00:12:58.649
impact on society, a major impact on healthcare,
and a major impact on the way we run our daily

00:12:58.649 --> 00:13:00.009
lives.

00:13:00.009 --> 00:13:04.970
I think this provides us with a whole lot
of new opportunities for providing a better

00:13:04.970 --> 00:13:11.860
urgent and emergency care system for the citizens
of this country, particularly at a time, as

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:19.040
you have heard from Barbara, when our accident
and emergency departments are under stress.

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:24.769
That stress does not originate in the accident
and emergency departments, who, frankly, you

00:13:24.769 --> 00:13:26.879
could argue, are victims of their own success.

00:13:26.879 --> 00:13:29.069
They provide a very good service, in my view.

00:13:29.069 --> 00:13:34.269
However, the problems of the totality of the
urgent and emergency care system are manifest

00:13:34.269 --> 00:13:36.459
in those departments.

00:13:36.459 --> 00:13:39.970
Reviewing urgent care
So I was asked by the secretary of state to

00:13:39.970 --> 00:13:45.619
undertake a review of the urgent and emergency
care system, and that has been led by one

00:13:45.619 --> 00:13:51.009
of my colleagues, Professor Keith Willett,
a professor of trauma and orthopaedics in

00:13:51.009 --> 00:13:52.009
Oxford.

00:13:52.009 --> 00:13:55.930
He and his team have reviewed the evidence
and have debated that evidence in equal measure

00:13:55.930 --> 00:14:00.339
with patients and with those who deliver the
service, who live and breathe the problems

00:14:00.339 --> 00:14:04.519
of the urgent and emergency care service on
a day-to-day basis.

00:14:04.519 --> 00:14:07.899
Publishing a vision
The result of that is that next week we will

00:14:07.899 --> 00:14:14.279
be publishing a vision for where we think
the service could move over the course of

00:14:14.279 --> 00:14:20.989
the next few years, to provide better care
for people in different circumstances, and

00:14:20.989 --> 00:14:28.399
where possible better care much closer to
home than is often the case at the moment.

00:14:28.399 --> 00:14:32.920
This is not going to be a detailed implementation
plan.

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:40.369
That will follow and will be led, I hope,
by a public debate, which we will share with

00:14:40.369 --> 00:14:48.269
people on NHS Choices and in the wider media,
who, in my view, have been extremely helpful

00:14:48.269 --> 00:14:53.910
in terms of enabling us to have an honest
and coherent debate about the provision of

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:58.480
seven-day services in our NHS, and I hope
that they can do the same with helping us

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:03.480
to redesign and redevelop our urgent and emergency
care services.

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:09.160
That will be published next week, and it will
be followed in three or four weeks by a letter

00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:19.910
to the Chairman of the Board on the deliberations
of a seven-day services forum, which I have

00:15:19.910 --> 00:15:27.069
been leading and which will hopefully start
to kick-start seven-day services in our National

00:15:27.069 --> 00:15:32.209
Health Service, but focusing in particular,
in the first instance, on urgent and emergency

00:15:32.209 --> 00:15:33.209
care.

00:15:33.209 --> 00:15:35.279
Reflections on winter
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Bruce,

00:15:35.279 --> 00:15:36.389
and thank you, Barbara.

00:15:36.389 --> 00:15:40.699
Let us just reflect on some of the messages
we have just heard.

00:15:40.699 --> 00:15:46.299
The issue of winter is clearly one where the
Board wants assurance that the system as a

00:15:46.299 --> 00:15:51.189
whole will work well together and that it
will best serve the interests of patients,

00:15:51.189 --> 00:15:57.329
but the way in which we are organising this
bears all the hallmarks of past experience.

00:15:57.329 --> 00:16:03.559
I would be really interested to know or to
try to explore with you, if you are reorganising

00:16:03.559 --> 00:16:11.609
this scenario around the role of NHS England
as a commissioning agency, how would you imagine

00:16:11.609 --> 00:16:14.269
this might look in, say, three years' time?

00:16:14.269 --> 00:16:15.389
Would it look the same?

00:16:15.389 --> 00:16:22.239
Would we still be doing the same oversight
and meticulous planning and working closely

00:16:22.239 --> 00:16:26.730
across the country with all these networks,
or would we have released more autonomy and

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:32.169
more authority to providers, and to CCGs,
and to have been able to step back a little,

00:16:32.169 --> 00:16:37.600
confident that our commissioning decisions
had accurately allocated resources to where

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:44.730
they were needed, and that the system could
work well without our meticulous and concentrated

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:45.730
oversight?

00:16:45.730 --> 00:16:50.220
Barbara Hakin: We certainly would see a very
different system, whereby local commissioners

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:54.629
and providers, overseen by the health and
wellbeing boards, and with the urgent care

00:16:54.629 --> 00:17:01.609
working groups, were much more able to take
full responsibility for ensuring that the

00:17:01.609 --> 00:17:03.339
plans were right in their own area.

00:17:03.339 --> 00:17:10.400
And I would think that hopefully by that time,
through the allocations process we would be

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:16.360
able to secure the funds for them at the beginning
of the year, and that they would not be dependent

00:17:16.360 --> 00:17:21.460
upon waiting to see whether, centrally, we
had more funds to allocate for winter.

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:26.890
So I think there would be a very significant
benefit in that, and I do think that once

00:17:26.890 --> 00:17:33.270
we have got the standards and more clear guidance,
and CCGs have become more mature organisations,

00:17:33.270 --> 00:17:39.110
and working with their partners obviously,
then I think we will see a situation where

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:43.660
locally they can ensure that they get the
right results.

00:17:43.660 --> 00:17:50.260
I think there will always be a place, however,
for NHS England working with its partners

00:17:50.260 --> 00:17:51.630
to do two things.

00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:56.780
One is to ensure that we do collect the information
and that we are clear what is happening, and

00:17:56.780 --> 00:18:01.730
therefore, through our national voice, are
able to reassure patients and the public,

00:18:01.730 --> 00:18:05.720
as well as the local reassurance that we would
expect CCGs to be giving to their patients

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:08.050
that these services are safe and effective.

00:18:08.050 --> 00:18:16.060
I think that there will always be certain
services that are delivered to a very small

00:18:16.060 --> 00:18:22.220
number of patients and that are very specialised,
where it will still be important that we just

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:29.220
keep the national oversight, because in terms
of things like paediatric intensive care,

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:31.050
I think that one has to have a national perspective.

00:18:31.050 --> 00:18:35.480
What is important, of course, is making sure
that we have the right perspective for each

00:18:35.480 --> 00:18:39.140
level, because some of these things will be
done regionally, some of these things will

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:43.200
be done across several CCGs, and then a small
number at national level.

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:50.160
Certainly, yes, I hope that we do not have
to keep putting the huge amount of effort

00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:55.340
that we have done at the moment overseeing
the plans that are coming up from the frontline,

00:18:55.340 --> 00:19:02.030
and we will be able to be confident that they
will be able to do these things themselves.

00:19:02.030 --> 00:19:12.600
Ciarán Devane: Maybe it is a version of the
same thing.

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:19.500
If we just take the short term and next year,
do we have to do the same thing?

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:22.500
Do we have to throw another £250 million
at things?

00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:27.960
How do we institutionalise things so that
it is much more business as usual?

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:36.740
Barbara Hakin: The NHS has always had extra
money for winter, and in a way, that itself

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:41.940
causes some issues, because the fact that
they always have had money for winter coming

00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:46.790
down means that they plan on that basis, and
I am sure that we would all absolutely agree

00:19:46.790 --> 00:19:51.220
that if we could be much more overt at the
beginning of the year and everybody was much

00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:55.250
clearer about what they could put in place
for winter and start planning sooner, that

00:19:55.250 --> 00:19:58.100
would be even better.

00:19:58.100 --> 00:20:02.260
This year, the government has committed not
only to the £250 million of additional funding

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:08.260
that we got this year, which it gave to NHS
England to distribute to the frontline, but

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:15.330
we have that commitment that that £250 million
will be available next winter as well.

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:20.180
In fact, we have been able to get that money
out to the frontline more swiftly than in

00:20:20.180 --> 00:20:25.740
the past, because often it has taken until
December for us to be able to identify that

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:27.490
we have spare funding.

00:20:27.490 --> 00:20:31.550
Certainly for those who have received the
funding, they are grateful that they have

00:20:31.550 --> 00:20:33.640
more time to put their plans in place.

00:20:33.640 --> 00:20:38.700
As I said before, I think in the ideal world,
there would not be this cycle; people would

00:20:38.700 --> 00:20:42.270
be very clear that they had the funds, and
we would be more confident the funds were

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:45.460
appropriately allocated in the first instance.

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:48.930
I know we are talking about allocations later
on.

00:20:48.930 --> 00:20:52.890
People would then take the responsibility
on themselves to make sure their community

00:20:52.890 --> 00:20:54.710
has the services.

00:20:54.710 --> 00:21:00.050
Malcolm Grant: Thank you both, Barbara and
Bruce, and we look forward to hearing more

00:21:00.050 --> 00:21:03.270
about the urgent and emergency care review
at a future meeting.

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:04.270
We have feedback from some six or seven committees
this morning, and I am going to invite the

00:21:04.270 --> 00:21:05.270
Chairs of the respective committees to speak
about the brief reports that we have in the

00:21:05.270 --> 00:21:06.270
papers.

00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:06.282
Ed, may we turn to you in relation to the
Audit committee?

